{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the complex, often bitter, relationships behind its creation. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie is less a biopic and more a modern tragedy about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached—a genius who builds a global empire but loses his humanity in the process.\n\nthe screenplay, written by aaron sorkin, is a tour de force of rapid-fire dialogue and layered storytelling. every line feels deliberate, every exchange a psychological duel. sorkin’s script weaves together multiple timelines—the harvard lawsuit depositions and the early days of facebook’s founding—creating a narrative that’s as much about legal battles as it is about personal alienation. the pacing never falters, and the tension builds like a thriller, even though the outcome is already public knowledge.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally compelling. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, the loyal friend turned adversary, conveying vulnerability and quiet anguish with remarkable subtlety. jesse plemons is quietly menacing as the winklevoss twins, a dual role handled with eerie symmetry. and jude law, as the slick, opportunistic attorney, brings a chilling charm to the courtroom scenes.\n\ndanny elfman’s score, though subtle, is hauntingly effective, blending electronic pulses with classical strings to mirror the coldness of technology and the warmth of lost friendships. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth uses cool blues and stark lighting to reinforce the isolation of the characters, even in crowded rooms.\n\nwhat makes the film truly enduring is its ambiguity. it doesn’t vilify zuckerberg outright, nor does it glorify him. instead, it presents him as a product of his environment—a boy who wanted to belong, and ended up building a platform that changed how the world connects, while remaining disconnected himself. the final scene, where he refreshes his own facebook page, hoping for a friend request from someone he lost, is devastating in its simplicity.\n\nthe social network isn’t just about facebook. it’s about how power, technology, and human emotion collide in the 21st century. it’s a film that feels prophetic, timely, and timeless all at once. a brilliant, unsettling, and unforgettable piece of cinema.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -1.2010457515716553}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.7369593381881714}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.10027764737606049}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.0411134250462055e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00035422726068645716}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.25446972250938416}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.2053474822314456e-05}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -2.0473005771636963}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.16627512872219086}, {"token": " often", "logprob": -0.005900938529521227}, {"token": " bitter", "logprob": -1.1219851970672607}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1790244579315186}, {"token": " relationships", "logprob": -0.9506688117980957}, {"token": " behind", "logprob": -